<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vedeno</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vedeno is a village in the mountains of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechnya">Chechnya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Imam Shamil</a>'s Caucasian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> after the loss of Dargo and was taken by Russian forces in April 1859. In 1919–1920 it was again the seat of an Islamic state, the North Caucasian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-haji">Uzun-Haji</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vedeno.jpg" alt="Vedeno" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oleg Belonenko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vedeno,_Chechnya,_Russia_-_panoramio_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vedeno is a village in the central part of Vedensky District, between the Akhkinchu and Khulkhulau rivers, 67 kilometres south-east of Grozny. It stands at an average altitude of 722 metres, and to the south the Khoroch ridge rises above it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 19th century Vedeno was the last capital of the Caucasian Imamate, an Islamic territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechnya">Chechnya</a> that existed from 1829 to 1859. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Imam Shamil</a> led his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> army in a twenty-year war against the Imperial Russian army and fought a last stand here. Russian forces captured the village on 1 April 1859 and built a fortress there. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> it was the administrative capital of the Vedensky Okrug of the Terek Oblast.</p><p>From September 1919 to March 1920 it was the capital of the North Caucasian Emirate, an Islamic state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechnya">Chechnya</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>. Money was printed for the emirate in Vedeno, on several kinds of paper, including pages from school books.</p><p>In 1944 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a> and Ingush were deported and the Chechen-Ingush ASSR was abolished. Vedeno was one of very few villages not renamed, but on 7 June 1944 its district was transferred to the Dagestan ASSR, with Vedeno as the district centre. In 1957, after the Vaynakh people returned, the district went back to the restored Chechen-Ingush ASSR.</p><p>During the First Chechen War Vedeno was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechnya">Chechen Republic</a> of Ichkeria until Russian troops took it on 3 June 1995 and held it to the end of the war. In the Second Chechen War, on 17 November 1999, the 31st Separate Airborne Brigade lost 12 paratroopers in a battle in the village, and Russian troops took it again on 11 January 2000. Separatists led by Ibn Khattab ambushed Russian troops there in August 2001, and in March 2006 fighting took place near the village.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vedeno is the administrative centre of Vedensky District and of the rural settlement of Vedenskoye. Its population was 3,186 at the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, about 70 per cent of them ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>, with Russians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezgins">Lezgins</a>, Tabasarans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumyks">Kumyks</a> among the others.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vedeno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The capital moved, settling at <b>Vedeno</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/caucasian-imamate">Caucasian Imamate</a></li><li><blockquote>After Denikin's Volunteer Army overran the Mountainous Republic of the Northern Caucasus in 1919, the elderly Naqshbandi sheikh Uzun-Haji, who had long called for a revival of Shamil's imamate, proclaimed the North Caucasian Emirate in September 1919 in the mountains of Chechnya, with its seat at <b>Vedeno</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-caucasian-emirate">North Caucasian Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
